---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'
import Arrow from '../../components/Arrow.astro'
import Consultation from '../../components/Consultation.astro'
const chapters = [
  {
    marker: '01',
    phase: 'School years',
    title: 'The kid who wanted to figure things out.',
    paragraphs: [
      "I didn't start with a job title or a grand plan. In seventh grade, I learned HTML and CSS from YouTube because I wanted to know how websites were made. Then I became curious about what else a computer could do.",
      'I tried Windows CMD, batch files, VBS, registry edits and little automations. I put together commands for making bootable USB drives, customized the right-click menu on my computer and built a tiny experiment I called Imager. I also played with converters, calculators, word counters and QR tools.',
      "Most of it wasn't for a client. Nobody was waiting for those tools. I liked taking a task apart, understanding it, and making it easier.",
    ],
  },
  {
    marker: '02',
    phase: 'RedsGlow / First job at 15',
    title: 'The first real test.',
    paragraphs: [
      'At 15 I started working at RedsGlow, then a startup. I worked on website speed, WordPress plugins, hosting, security and the technical problems that come with keeping multiple sites running.',
      "One day a WordPress site crashed while I was in the office. The regular developer wasn't available and the team was stuck. I offered to investigate, researched the problem and worked through the fix. The website came back online.",
      "That moment stayed with me. I didn't know the answer when I raised my hand. I just knew I could learn enough to help. It's still how I approach unfamiliar problems.",
    ],
  },
  {
    marker: '03',
    phase: 'Pearl Lemon / SEO',
    title: 'From SEO intern to leading delivery.',
    paragraphs: [
      'I joined Pearl Lemon on April 29, 2024 as an Off-Page SEO Intern. The work grew quickly. I learned how outreach, links, technical decisions and client expectations fit into a wider search strategy.',
      'I eventually became Head of Off-Page SEO for the client-facing side, handling 15 or more campaigns at once, sometimes over 20. I trained colleagues, improved processes and built small internal tools to take repetition out of the work.',
      'SEO taught me how businesses operate: not just how a page ranks, but how teams communicate, customers search and decisions get made.',
    ],
  },
  {
    marker: '04',
    phase: 'US agency partnership',
    title: 'Learning that the job is bigger than the title.',
    paragraphs: [
      'I also worked with Tracemark Impression, a US-based agency, helping coordinate local SEO for more than 20 clients. It was another opportunity to think about delivery at scale, with a team supporting the work.',
      "When the agency needed video editors, I helped find and coordinate a team in Pakistan. I wasn't becoming a video editor. I was solving a practical business problem using people, organization and whatever I could learn.",
    ],
  },
  {
    marker: '05',
    phase: 'The turning point',
    title: 'A weekend challenge changed everything.',
    paragraphs: [
      "At Pearl Lemon, CEO Deepak Shukla shared a challenge in our company group: build a useful tool to solve a problem over the weekend. I took it on and showed him what I'd made. I don't remember the first tool's exact name well enough to invent one.",
      "What mattered was what happened next. Deepak wanted me working directly with him. There wasn't a neatly defined new position waiting. I trained a successor, handed over the SEO work during a transition of roughly a month and moved toward a different kind of responsibility.",
      "The trust to help build something from its earliest days remains one of the moments I'm proudest of. That next chapter became LemStudio.",
    ],
  },
  {
    marker: '06',
    phase: 'LemStudio / Head of SaaS',
    title: 'Products, people and everything after the launch.',
    paragraphs: [
      "At LemStudio, I started building software around everyday business problems: scheduling, subscription tracking, search workflows, reports and operations. AI-assisted development became central to the way I work. I'm not a traditional programmer, and I don't claim to be. I start with the problem, shape the workflow, build with AI tools and development platforms, test it and keep improving what people use.",
      "My work has covered more than 50 software projects, including internal tools, prototypes, experiments and products that didn't all continue. Approximately 20 or more reached launch. That doesn't make every project a separate SaaS company, and the products belong to LemStudio.",
      'Some examples in that ecosystem are LemCal, LemSubs, LemWatch, LemForms, LemRank, LemTask, LemAudit and LemReveal. LemCal came from managing scheduling across more than 100 booking pages; LemSubs began with the headache of keeping track of recurring charges. I mention these as part of my experience, not as products I own personally.',
      "The work goes beyond building an initial version. I've handled integrations, migrations, permissions, billing questions, product feedback, launches, marketing and operational issues. I've also managed teams of more than ten and trained more than 25 people. A useful product needs people who can support it, improve it and get it into the hands of customers.",
    ],
  },
  {
    marker: '07',
    phase: 'What comes next',
    title: 'Still curious. More responsibility.',
    paragraphs: [
      'My work now sits across SaaS development, AI automation and SEO strategy. They might look like different fields, but the thread connecting them is the same thing that first interested me in computers: finding a problem and making something work better.',
      "I want to work with clients and partners who value thoughtful execution, and one day build my own business around software and SEO services. For now, I'm focused on learning, leading and doing work that stands up beyond a demo.",
      "Outside the work, I write poetry, read, cook and play cricket with friends. The curiosity that got me started is still there. I'd like to keep it that way.",
    ],
  },
]
---

<Layout
  title="My Story — Zain Saleem"
  description="From learning to build websites in seventh grade to leading SaaS, AI automation and SEO work: Zain Saleem's personal and professional journey."
>
  <section class="section page-intro brand-intro">
    <div class="container brand-lede">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">My story / In my own words</p>
        <h1>
          Nothing began
          <br />
          <span class="serif">with a title.</span>
        </h1>
      </div>
      <div class="brand-intro-copy">
        <p class="large-copy">
          I was curious about how things worked long before I knew what I wanted
          to do for a living.
        </p>
        <p>
          That curiosity took me from small computer experiments to SEO, leading
          teams and building SaaS products with AI. It hasn't always been a
          straight line—but to me it makes sense.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/experience/">
          View the professional timeline <Arrow diagonal />
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container brand-story-layout">
      <aside class="story-side">
        <p class="eyebrow">A story in seven chapters</p>
        <p>
          Curiosity → technical work → SEO leadership → SaaS and AI automation.
          The same instinct, with bigger problems to solve.
        </p>
      </aside>
      <div class="story-chapters">
        {chapters.map((chapter) => (
          <article class="story-chapter">
            <div class="story-chapter-meta">
              <span>{chapter.marker}</span>
              <span>{chapter.phase}</span>
            </div>
            <h2>{chapter.title}</h2>
            <div class="brand-reading">
              {chapter.paragraphs.map((paragraph) => (
                <p>{paragraph}</p>
              ))}
            </div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Consultation />
</Layout>
